WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.810
The shogun's army could not get into a single castle; inside there were neither soldiers nor walls, only peasants about to starve to death.

2
00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:12.900
And those castle walls were shelled by the Dutch, who wanted to trade with Japan.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:18.150
The door of a country that would stay shut for two hundred years slammed there, that winter.

4
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:35.600
In the southwest of Japan, there is a peninsula that hangs down from the coast of the island of Kyushu toward the Ariake Sea, built on the slopes of a volcanic mountain.

5
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.290
Its name is Shimabara.

6
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:50.260
Its soil is fertile, but it is not wide enough; the wind that blows off the sea is salty, the mountain is called Unzen, and near its summit there are pits where scalding water gushes up out of the ground as if boiling.

7
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:56.470
At the beginning of the seventeenth century, this peninsula was one of the most Christianized corners of Japan.

8
00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:06.360
The lords of the peninsula, the Arima family, had been baptized, had opened their doors to the missionaries, and churches and seminaries had been established in the villages.

9
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:13.370
By some estimates, the greater part of the peninsula's population had embraced the faith brought by the Portuguese Jesuits.

10
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.900
Then everything was turned on its head.

11
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:24.670
In sixteen fourteen, the shogunate banned Christianity across the whole country, sent the missionaries into exile, and had the churches torn down.

12
00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:31.040
In sixteen sixteen, the Arima family was removed from the peninsula and transferred to another province.

13
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.010
The man who came in their place was Matsukura Shigemasa.

14
00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:45.860
The territory that fell into Shigemasa's hands was, on paper, a domain of sixty thousand koku — that is, a place capable of producing sixty thousand measures of rice a year.

15
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:49.510
For a samurai house, this was a middling income.

16
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:53.800
Shigemasa, however, had no wish to be a middling man.

17
00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:01.930
In sixteen eighteen, on the flat ground along the seashore, he began building a castle that far exceeded the means of his own domain.

18
00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.140
It took seven years.

19
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:15.630
When it was finished, the circuit of the walls reached three thousand nine hundred metres, the walls in places rose fifteen metres high, moats between thirty and fifty metres wide encircled the castle,

20
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.280
and sixteen towers had been set at the key points.

21
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.410
The five-storey main keep was visible from every part of the peninsula.

22
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:29.140
According to the reckonings of the time, the cost of this castle was twice what the domain could bear.

23
00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.150
The difference was taken out of the peasants.

24
00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:38.520
Tax rates were raised so high that they bore no relation at all to the domain's real productive capacity.

25
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:48.890
New items were added on top of the rice tax: a hearth tax, a window tax, a tax for every child born, a burial tax for every person who died.

26
00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:53.180
For those who could not pay, the penalty was not a fine.

27
00:02:53.180 --> 00:03:02.910
According to the records of the period, a peasant unable to pay his taxes would be dressed in a straw raincoat, his arms bound behind him, and the raincoat set alight;

28
00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:09.120
this torture was given the name "the straw raincoat dance," after the movements of a man writhing in the flames.

29
00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:16.130
The wives and daughters of those who could not pay were hung upside down and plunged into icy water in the middle of winter.

30
00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:20.420
Taxation and religious persecution were joined in the same hand.

31
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:29.350
From sixteen twenty-seven onward, the Matsukura family began throwing those who refused to renounce Christianity into the boiling waters of Unzen.

32
00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:37.480
Those who were caught would be taken up the mountain, held for days in the stench of sulphur, and brought one by one to the edge of the pit to make them give it up.

33
00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.450
Some were crucified, some were burned alive.

34
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:52.780
Nor was Shigemasa content with that; believing that the source of Christianity had to be dried up, he drew up a plan for an expedition against the island of Luzon in Spanish hands — that is,

35
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:59.630
the north of what is today the Philippines — and the cost of this expedition, too, he demanded from his peasants.

36
00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:03.120
The expedition never took place.

37
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.570
Shigemasa died in sixteen thirty, in a hot spring town.

38
00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:11.700
He was succeeded by his son, Matsukura Katsuie.

39
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.830
He did not change a single line of his father's policy.

40
00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:21.720
Meanwhile, in Nagasaki, to the north of the peninsula, a new practice had begun.

41
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:29.690
From sixteen twenty-nine onward, the people of the city were ordered, once a year, to step on a medallion or a plaque placed before them.

42
00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.300
On the plaque was an image of the Virgin Mary or of Jesus.

43
00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:46.270
This was called "the trampled picture." Merchant, monk, samurai, woman, child; they would line up, lift a foot before the eyes of an official, step on the image, and pass on.

44
00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:50.880
Whoever stepped was cleared; whoever hesitated was written down on a list.

45
00:04:50.880 --> 00:05:00.930
This method, which set out to measure a human being's inner world by the movement of a foot, was the beginning of a system of screening that would last in Japan for more than two hundred years.

46
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.980
Then came the famine.

47
00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:12.230
A run of bad harvests in the middle of the sixteen thirties drove both the Shimabara Peninsula and the Amakusa islands on the opposite shore into starvation.

48
00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:18.440
The peasant's granary was empty, his seed grain eaten, and the tax collector was at the door.

49
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.770
Requests for tax relief were turned down.

50
00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.380
The domain's collectors went on making their rounds from village to village.

51
00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:38.030
On the seventeenth of December in the year sixteen thirty-seven, in the village of Arima in the south of the peninsula, the district tax collector Hayashi Hyozaemon found himself surrounded by peasants and was killed.

52
00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:41.840
This did not remain merely the news of a murder.

53
00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:47.890
Word spread across the peninsula within a day, and the next day the neighbouring villages rose up as well.

54
00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:58.260
The peasants who brought out the spears, axes and sickles they had hidden along the coast were joined by former warriors who had lost their masters thirty years earlier and had lived by farming ever since.

55
00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.270
The uprising began as a tax revolt.

56
00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:04.760
Within a week, it had turned into something else.

57
00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:09.050
The person who took charge of the crowd was sixteen years old.

58
00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:14.940
His name was Masuda Shiro Tokisada, but history remembers him as Amakusa Shiro.

59
00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:27.550
His father, Masuda Jinbei, had once served a Christian lord; after that lord was destroyed, he was left a warrior without land, and he raised his son on the Amakusa islands, in a world shaped by missionaries.

60
00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.040
The boy was baptised with the name Jerome.

61
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:38.850
He could read and write, he spoke well, and in the first days of the uprising he became both the rebels' council of command and their symbol.

62
00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.660
It was not talent alone that carried him to that position.

63
00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:56.550
For years a prophecy had passed from mouth to mouth across the peninsula: one of the exiled missionaries, it was said, had foretold that twenty-five years after the persecution began a youth would rise in the land,

64
00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:00.680
that he would be sixteen years old, and that he would gather the people.

65
00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.050
The arithmetic fitted.

66
00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:08.780
To a population worn down by hunger and torture, this boy was more than a commander.

67
00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:14.190
By some accounts, stories circulated around him that miracles came from his hands.

68
00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:24.880
Whether or not they were true hardly mattered to the course of events; the people believed, and that belief was a stubbornness sufficient to hold out for three months against an army of a hundred and twenty thousand.

69
00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.690
The rebels' first move was to march on two castles.

70
00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:34.900
At Shimabara they laid siege to the five-storey castle raised with the blood and labour of the peasants.

71
00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:36.790
They could not take it.

72
00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:45.080
There was no way to capture a structure with walls fifteen metres high and moats fifty metres wide using farm tools and a handful of muskets.

73
00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:56.250
On the opposite shore they besieged Tomioka Castle on the Amakusa islands; at one point they were almost inside, and then reinforcements from the domain's forces arrived and they were forced to fall back.

74
00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.580
Not a single castle had been won.

75
00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:07.550
Meanwhile the shogunate's order to the domains of Kyushu — raise troops at once and crush the rising — had long since gone out.

76
00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:19.680
And it was at that point that the rebels made one of the most obstinate defensive decisions in history: instead of looking for a castle they could take, they chose to shut themselves inside a castle nobody wanted.

77
00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:25.250
On the southern coast of the peninsula, on a hill looking out to sea, stood an abandoned ruin.

78
00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:28.100
Its name was Hara Castle.

79
00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:37.990
It had been founded in fourteen ninety-six by Arima Takazumi of the Arima family, and at the end of the sixteenth century it was rebuilt from top to bottom by Arima Harunobu.

80
00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:45.960
In that work Harunobu drew on the castle architecture he had seen during the Korean campaigns, ringing the inner keep with stone walls;

81
00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:53.130
Jesuit records confirm that the stone ramparts of the main inner keep were raised between fifteen ninety-nine and sixteen oh four.

82
00:08:53.130 --> 00:09:03.660
But when the Arima family left the region in sixteen sixteen the castle was emptied: the roofs caved in, the timber fittings were stripped away, grass grew up between the walls.

83
00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.790
For twenty years it was a heap of stone no one came near.

84
00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:10.160
The rebels poured into it.

85
00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:21.650
The villagers of the Shimabara Peninsula, and those who crossed by boat from the Amakusa islands, climbed the hill — first with their families, then with their livestock, and finally with everything they could carry.

86
00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:27.860
All together, counting women, the old and the children, there were some thirty-seven thousand of them.

87
00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:31.670
Of these, perhaps a third could bear arms.

88
00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:35.480
Within three weeks they turned that ruin into a fortress.

89
00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:44.250
They shored up the collapsed walls with earth and with planks torn from village houses, they cleared the moats, they drove palisades into the paths leading up the hill.

90
00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:48.380
The sheer face above the sea they left as a natural rampart.

91
00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:52.350
They counted the rice they had brought inside and put it on ration.

92
00:09:52.350 --> 00:10:02.240
This was the most critical calculation of the siege, and though no one said it aloud, the sum was bad: for thirty-seven thousand people, the stores were measured in months.

93
00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.130
They also made a banner.

94
00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:12.100
The man who painted it was named Yamada Emosaku, an artist who had learned painting from European missionaries and knew how to prepare pigments.

95
00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:21.830
At the centre of the standard he hung on the castle walls he set a chalice and two angels, and above them he wrote a sentence in Portuguese: praised be the Most Holy Sacrament.

96
00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:27.880
That banner is today one of the most striking objects preserved in Japan to have survived the siege.

97
00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:33.290
And the story of the man who painted it would arrive at an unexpected place in the castle's fall.

98
00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:37.740
On the shogunate's side, the matter was being taken seriously.

99
00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:46.670
The government in Edo ordered every domain in Kyushu to put troops in the field and placed an official named Itakura Shigemasa in charge of suppressing the rising.

100
00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:53.520
Towards the end of January, the whole stretch of land on the castle's landward side was covered with army camps.

101
00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:58.450
The siege formally began on the twenty-second of January, sixteen thirty-eight.

102
00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:03.220
Itakura Shigemasa underestimated what stood in front of him.

103
00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:13.110
He asked himself how many days a crowd of mostly farmers, crammed into a ruined castle, could last against regular forces gathered from some of the greatest domains in Japan,

104
00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:18.680
and he reckoned the answer at "not many." On the third of February he launched the first assault.

105
00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:20.890
The plan failed.

106
00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:29.020
The rebels had narrowed the castle's few points of access, arranging their defences so that an attacker could advance only along a narrow front.

107
00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:38.910
The forces climbing the hill were met with stones, logs and boiling water hurled from above; among the defenders, musketeers opened fire at close range.

108
00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:41.280
The first assault was broken.

109
00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.890
Itakura tried again on the fourteenth of February.

110
00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:52.740
The combined toll of the two attacks was more than six hundred men lost, and not a single breach opened in the walls.

111
00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:57.990
In the course of the second assault, the commander himself was shot, and died.

112
00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:05.640
The first supreme commander the shogunate had sent to the peninsula had lost his life before a peasant uprising, on the slope of a ruin.

113
00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:10.250
Edo's response to this news was a change of method.

114
00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:14.540
Matsudaira Nobutsuna was appointed in Itakura's place.

115
00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:19.790
The new commander did the exact opposite of his predecessor: he stopped attacking.

116
00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:27.280
He had ditches dug around the castle, breastworks raised, watchtowers erected, and mine tunnels driven beneath the walls.

117
00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:34.130
With soldiers flowing in from the surrounding domains, the strength of the camp passed one hundred and twenty-five thousand.

118
00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:38.100
He lined boats along the seaward side of the castle.

119
00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:42.390
Now he had only one aim: that nothing at all should get inside.

120
00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:44.760
He also asked for cannon.

121
00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:56.090
Japan had cannon, but what Nobutsuna wanted was firepower heavy enough to pound a hilltop, and the nearest source of that was the Dutch trading post on the island of Hirado, north of the peninsula.

122
00:12:56.090 --> 00:13:05.660
The Dutch wanted to strengthen their position against the Portuguese, the only other Europeans permitted to trade in Japan; refusing a request from the shogunate was simply unthinkable.

123
00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:14.910
Nicolaes Couckebacker, the man heading the Dutch East India Company at Hirado, first sent cannon and powder ashore; these were formed into a battery on the coast.

124
00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.600
Then the shogunate asked for a ship.

125
00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:25.410
Couckebacker personally took the twenty-gun sailing ship named de Ryp — the Ryp, for short — out to the waters off Hara Castle.

126
00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:32.900
The ship took up position on the twenty-fourth of February and bombarded the castle for fifteen days, until the twelfth of March.

127
00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:37.350
In that time it fired roughly four hundred and twenty-six rounds.

128
00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.520
The result was very nearly nothing.

129
00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:52.170
The castle stood atop a steep hill overlooking the sea, and most of the shot fired from the ship's deck sailed over the target and fell behind the hill — into the camp of the very Japanese army conducting the siege.

130
00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:55.500
No serious breach was opened in the walls.

131
00:13:55.500 --> 00:14:03.310
The rebels, for their part, returned fire at the ship; two Dutchmen standing watch at the Ryp's lookout mast were shot and killed.

132
00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:09.840
But the greatest damage the Ryp did to the siege came not from the shot it fired, but from its mere presence.

133
00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:14.610
The rebels lowered mocking messages from the walls to the besieging army.

134
00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:21.620
The gist of them was this: are there no brave men left in this country to fight us, that you must call foreigners to your aid?

135
00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:30.230
For an army of one hundred and twenty-five thousand samurai that had come to put down a rebellion, this was a question that touched the tenderest spot of all.

136
00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:34.040
The Japanese commanders asked the Dutch to withdraw.

137
00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.570
The Ryp weighed anchor and left.

138
00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:41.500
For the remainder of the siege, no foreign gun fired on the castle walls again.

139
00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:46.590
Once the foreign ship was gone, what remained was Nobutsuna's real weapon.

140
00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:51.040
That weapon was the siege itself, and it was working in silence.

141
00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.810
Through the month of March, nothing much happened on the walls of Hara Castle.

142
00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:58.340
What was happening was inside it.

143
00:14:58.340 --> 00:15:06.790
Thirty-seven thousand people, on a hilltop sealed off from every direction but the sea, were living on rations they had calculated back in January.

144
00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:10.280
In the middle of March the rice ran out.

145
00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:13.450
Then the animals left to slaughter ran out.

146
00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:18.060
Then the grasses on the hill were pulled up and the bark was stripped from the trees.

147
00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:25.390
In the end they began climbing down the steep face of the castle to gather seaweed from the rocks the tide had left bare.

148
00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:31.920
The besiegers could see this; they watched the people coming down from a distance, and they knew what they were collecting.

149
00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:36.370
At the beginning of April the rebels attempted a night sortie.

150
00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:43.860
A detachment came out of the castle and raided the siege lines; the aim was both to seize provisions and to disrupt the siege works.

151
00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:48.790
The raid was broken, and a large part of those who came out died at the lines.

152
00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:53.240
And it was over those dead that the coldest scene of the whole siege took place.

153
00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:57.370
Matsudaira Nobutsuna ordered the bellies of the corpses opened.

154
00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:00.860
The point was not revenge, but intelligence.

155
00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:09.310
The commander wanted to know how far the starvation inside the castle had gone, and the surest way to learn that was to look inside the enemy's stomach.

156
00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:14.560
In the stomachs they opened there was no rice, no meat, no grain.

157
00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:18.050
There was only seaweed, grass and tree bark.

158
00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:22.180
That single finding answered every question Nobutsuna had.

159
00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:28.870
The castle's provisions were exhausted; inside, the number of people with any strength left to fight was falling by the day.

160
00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:32.200
Preparations for the final assault began.

161
00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:37.130
Before the preparations were complete, one more name entered the story.

162
00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:43.660
Yamada Emosaku, the painter who had drawn the castle's banner, made contact with the besieging army.

163
00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:50.510
He informed the besiegers of the situation inside the castle, which point was weak, and how the defence was coming apart.

164
00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:58.000
When the rebels realised this, they seized him to be put to death; the man was awaiting his execution as the castle fell.

165
00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.770
On the twelfth of April, sixteen thirty-eight, the final assault began.

166
00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:14.900
Even its beginning was unplanned: a fire that broke out along the siege line, or a signal misread, dragged one domain's forces up the slope before their time, and when the others refused to be left behind,

167
00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:17.910
line after line marched for the hill.

168
00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:23.640
What emerged was not the orderly assault Nobutsuna had designed, but a scattered and tangled rush.

169
00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:28.730
This time, though, what faced them was a defence that had been living on seaweed for months.

170
00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:32.700
The outer lines of defence fell on the first day.

171
00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:36.670
Inside, they fought for three days.

172
00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:43.520
Pressed in among the walls, using the last of their musket powder and the last of their stones, they resisted street by street.

173
00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:50.210
The forces that broke into the castle found, alongside the fighters, women, the old and children.

174
00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:55.300
The sources do not describe those three days in detail; the numbers describe them.

175
00:17:55.300 --> 00:18:01.670
The siege army's losses across the whole siege, dead and wounded together, come to around twenty thousand.

176
00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:08.680
Of the thirty-seven thousand people inside the castle, almost no one survived the battle and the executions that followed it.

177
00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:12.650
Amakusa Shiro was killed during the assault.

178
00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:18.060
His head was cut off, carried to Nagasaki and displayed to the public on the point of a spear.

179
00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:27.150
The severed head of the sixteen-year-old boy who had become the symbol of the uprising was left at the entrance to the city for days, like a warning sign.

180
00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:32.560
The castle fell completely on the fifteenth of April, sixteen thirty-eight.

181
00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:39.890
The stone walls were pulled down and the rubble was tipped into the ditches, leaving the site on the peninsula unusable ever again.

182
00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:49.620
The only person recorded as surviving the siege of Hara Castle was the painter Yamada Emosaku, who had drawn the castle's banner and then passed its secrets to the outside.

183
00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.830
The shogunate pardoned him.

184
00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:55.960
The flag he had painted was recovered from the rubble and preserved.

185
00:18:55.960 --> 00:19:01.690
Once the castle had fallen, it was the turn of the side that had provoked the revolt to answer for it.

186
00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:08.860
Matsukura Katsuie had not been on the peninsula during the uprising; as the shogunate's system required, he was in Edo.

187
00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:15.550
After the siege ended, the state of his domain was investigated, and the picture that emerged appalled the shogunate.

188
00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:21.440
In May of sixteen thirty-eight his domain was stripped from him; during the investigation,

189
00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:28.610
the discovery of a dead peasant inside his own residence was enough to document what had been said about his methods of tax collection.

190
00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:34.980
Katsuie was summoned to Edo, tried, and on the twenty-eighth of August, sixteen thirty-eight, beheaded.

191
00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:38.950
This punishment has no equal in Japan's recent history.

192
00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:50.280
Across roughly three centuries of the Edo period there were many lords who fell from favour, lost their domains, were exiled; but a lord being put to death by beheading happened only once.

193
00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:59.370
A samurai, even for the gravest crime, was granted the right to cut open his own belly; that right was the last door left open to a nobleman's honour.

194
00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:02.700
For Katsuie, that door did not open.

195
00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:12.750
In this way the shogunate was declaring something: that a lord crushing his people hard enough to drive his own domain into revolt was, in the eyes of the ruling house, not an error but a crime.

196
00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:19.920
Shimabara was given to a new lord, Koriki Tadafusa, and the tax rates were brought down to a reasonable level.

197
00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:24.370
But there was no one left on the peninsula to pay the tax.

198
00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:30.740
Most of the population had died at Hara Castle; the villages were empty, the fields unploughed.

199
00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:36.790
The shogunate resettled the peninsula by moving families to Shimabara from other parts of Japan.

200
00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:42.680
What mattered in choosing the newcomers was plain: they would come from regions that were not Christian.

201
00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:46.970
Because this was the real conclusion Edo had drawn from the uprising.

202
00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:57.820
The shogunate read the people who had held out for three months on the walls of Hara Castle, feeding on seaweed, not as a tax revolt but as a problem of loyalty created by a foreign faith.

203
00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:05.310
It had seen that, as long as that faith remained in the country, its own subjects could hold out this long against its own army.

204
00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:09.920
And to remove that possibility, it closed the doors.

205
00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:15.650
In sixteen thirty-nine, Portuguese merchants were banned from entering Japan altogether.

206
00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:21.060
The crew of one Portuguese delegation that tried to come despite the ban was executed.

207
00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:33.510
In sixteen forty-one, the Dutch — the only Europeans still permitted to remain in Japan — were taken from their settlement at Hirado and moved to an artificial, fan-shaped island built in the middle of Nagasaki harbour.

208
00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:38.280
The island was called Dejima, and it was about the size of a football pitch.

209
00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:44.330
For two hundred years, all of Japan's contact with the West passed across the bridge of that small island.

210
00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:52.780
The annual ceremony of treading on the image, practised in Nagasaki, turned after the uprising from a local check into a nationwide routine.

211
00:21:52.780 --> 00:22:02.190
In the villages of Kyushu an official would come every year, lay the plaque on the ground, and have everyone, household by household, step on it in turn.

212
00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:14.480
Even so, the faith did not disappear: in these regions of Japan, communities left without priests, without churches, without books passed their prayers down from memory, generation to generation,

213
00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:18.290
and lived in hiding for more than two hundred years.

214
00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:25.300
When missionaries came to Nagasaki again in the middle of the nineteenth century, villagers stepped forward to introduce themselves.

215
00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:30.230
The Shimabara Rebellion had one more consequence, and no one had planned it.

216
00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:36.920
The fall of Hara Castle turned out to be the last major armed conflict Japan would see for two hundred and thirty years.

217
00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:43.610
From sixteen thirty-eight until the civil war of eighteen sixty-eight, no war on that scale occurred again.

218
00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:51.900
Japan's famous long peace, in truth, begins on the slope of a ruin, with three days in which thirty-seven thousand people died.

219
00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:59.710
As for the doors that closed, they were forced open in eighteen fifty-three, when American warships dropped anchor in Edo Bay.

220
00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:02.720
Today there is no castle on that hill.

221
00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:08.130
Because its rubble was dumped into the moats, the site was used as farmland for centuries.

222
00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:15.140
In nineteen thirty-eight the area was declared a national historic site, and then the excavations began.

223
00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:24.550
What came out of the ground gave a more tangible account of the siege than the written records did: musket balls, fragments of cannon shot, broken cooking pots,

224
00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:32.040
skeletons buried at the foot of the walls — and among those skeletons, medallions, crosses, rosaries.

225
00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:38.890
In an excavation in the year two thousand, the remains of one of the largest castle gates in Japan were uncovered.

226
00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:47.020
In twenty eighteen the site was added to the World Heritage list, along with other places belonging to the hidden Christian heritage of the Nagasaki region.

227
00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:54.190
Anyone who climbs that hill looking out over the Ariake Sea today sees only grass, a line of stones, and the horizon.

228
00:23:54.190 --> 00:24:03.760
But to know that Japan's decision to close itself off from the world for two hundred years was set in motion from this silent slope changes the way you see the view.

229
00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:07.410
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

230
00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:11.540
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
